      text: "For most of the twentieth century, a diagnosis of a serious genetic disorder was effectively a life sentence. Treatment could ease the symptoms, but the underlying cause, a single faulty instruction in the patient's DNA, could not be touched. That assumption is now being overturned.\n\nIn recent years, gene editing has been transformed from a laboratory curiosity into a clinical reality. Using tools that act like molecular scissors, researchers can cut the genome at a precise location and either disable a harmful gene or switch on a helpful one. In the first clinical trials for inherited blood disorders, stem cells were removed from each patient's bone marrow, edited in the laboratory and then returned to the body. Months later, most participants no longer needed regular blood transfusions, and several have now been in remission for years.\n\nSuch results are rightly described as groundbreaking, but they should be interpreted with caution. Trial cohorts were small, often fewer than fifty people, and long-term data are still being collected. It is not yet known whether edits made today could cause unexpected problems decades from now. Every new therapy must therefore pass rigorous peer review and be monitored closely after approval.\n\nGene editing is not the only frontier. Immunotherapy, in which a patient's own immune cells are reprogrammed to recognise and attack tumours, has turned some previously untreatable cancers into curable or at least manageable chronic conditions. Meanwhile, scientists are learning to grow tissue from stem cells, raising hopes that damaged hearts and spinal cords might one day be encouraged to regenerate. Advanced prosthetic limbs, controlled directly by signals from the brain, are already being fitted in specialist centres.\n\nYet the picture is not uniformly bright. Antibiotic resistance is spreading so fast that common infections may once again become deadly; it has been estimated that resistant pathogens are already directly responsible for more than a million deaths a year. Developing new antibiotics is expensive and commercially unattractive, so far fewer are being produced than are needed.\n\nThere is also the question of cost. The first gene therapies have been priced at well over a million dollars per patient, which means that, for now, they are available only in wealthy countries. Critics argue that a cure which cannot be afforded is hardly a cure at all.\n\nMedicine has always advanced in leaps rather than in a straight line. If the efficacy of today's experimental treatments is confirmed and their price falls, the coming decades may be remembered as the moment when diseases that had been feared for centuries were finally eradicated.",
