      text: "'If you are not paying for the product, you are the product.' The phrase has become something of a cliché, yet it captures a reality that most internet users still underestimate. Every search, purchase and click contributes to a digital footprint which can be harvested, analysed and sold, frequently to third parties whose names the user has never encountered.\n\nResearch consistently indicates a gap between what people say about privacy and how they behave online. In surveys, a substantial majority of respondents describe themselves as concerned about how companies handle their personal data. Nevertheless, very few read the terms and conditions before accepting them, and fewer still adjust their settings to opt out of tracking cookies. Scholars refer to this phenomenon as the 'privacy paradox'. One plausible explanation is that the costs of sharing data are abstract and delayed, whereas the benefits, such as free services and personalised recommendations, are immediate and tangible.\n\nThe business model underlying this exchange is straightforward. Platforms monetise attention by selling targeted advertising, and the more precisely they can profile users, the higher the price advertisers are willing to pay. Even seemingly harmless metadata, such as the time and location of a message, can reveal a great deal: studies have demonstrated that a person's home address, daily routine and social circle can be inferred from location data alone, even when it has supposedly been anonymised.\n\nThe risks are not merely theoretical. Large-scale data breaches have exposed the passwords, medical records and financial details of hundreds of millions of people, providing cybercriminals with the raw material for fraud and identity theft. Meanwhile, the spread of facial recognition and other biometric technologies has prompted concern that routine surveillance is gradually eroding the very notion of a private sphere.\n\nLegislators have responded with varying degrees of determination. The European Union's General Data Protection Regulation, which came into force in 2018, requires organisations to have a lawful basis, such as explicit consent, for processing personal data, to report serious breaches within 72 hours and to respect individuals' right to be forgotten. Companies found to infringe these rules face fines of up to four per cent of their global turnover. Critics argue, however, that enforcement remains inconsistent and that compliance often amounts to little more than an additional pop-up window that users dismiss without reading.\n\nIt would therefore be naive to assume that regulation alone can resolve the problem. Greater transparency, stronger default settings and better digital literacy are all required if individuals are to regain meaningful control over their information. Until then, the price of 'free' services is likely to remain considerably higher than it appears.",
